          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Why is the term 'developmental dysplasia of the hip' (DDH) preferred over the ol</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Why is the term 'developmental dysplasia of the hip' (DDH) preferred over the older term 'congenital dislocation of the hip' (CDH)?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Because the condition is always bilateral and therefore not a simple dislocation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DDH is unilateral in the majority of cases. The change in terminology is unrelated to laterality — it reflects the dynamic nature of the condition.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Because some hips are only subluxable at birth and progress to dislocation postnatally</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. 'Developmental' acknowledges that the abnormality may not be a frank dislocation at birth — some hips are subluxable and progress to dislocation in the early postnatal weeks if undetected.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Because the dislocation is acquired during the birth process rather than present in utero</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>DDH is not caused by birth trauma. The term reflects that the condition encompasses a spectrum (dysplasia → subluxation → dislocation) that can evolve after birth.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Because the acetabulum is structurally normal at birth in all affected infants</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In DDH the acetabulum is typically shallow (dysplastic) from early development. The 'developmental' label reflects the dynamic progression, not a normal initial anatomy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key reason is that some hips are subluxable (not yet fully dislocated) at birth and worsen postnatally if undetected. This is why early screening with the Ortolani and Barlow tests is critical."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The 'developmental' label captures both the spectrum of severity and the potential for postnatal progression — which is why neonatal screening aims to detect subluxable hips before they become irreducibly dislocated."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
