          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>During the Adam's forward bend test, what clinical finding confirms that a scoli</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>During the Adam's forward bend test, what clinical finding confirms that a scoliosis is structural rather than postural?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>A rib hump becomes visible on the convex side of the curve</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Vertebral rotation — the hallmark of structural scoliosis — pushes the ribs posteriorly on the convex side, producing a visible rib hump on forward bending. Postural curves lack this rotation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The lateral spinal curve disappears completely on forward bending</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Disappearance of the curve on bending indicates a non-structural (postural) scoliosis. In structural scoliosis the curve persists because it is maintained by vertebral rotation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The patient reports sharp pain along the convex side of the curve</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pain is not the distinguishing feature of structural scoliosis. Most adolescent idiopathic scoliosis is painless; the presence of pain actually raises concern for other underlying pathology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The Cobb angle measures less than 10° on standing radiograph</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A Cobb angle below 10° does not meet the radiographic definition of scoliosis. The Adam's test is a clinical bedside examination; Cobb angle is measured on a standing AP radiograph.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In structural scoliosis, vertebral rotation causes the ribs to protrude posteriorly on the convex side, producing a visible rib hump when the patient bends forward. Postural curves lack this rotation and disappear with bending or lying down."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Precisely. The rib hump on forward bending reflects underlying vertebral rotation — the pathological hallmark that distinguishes true structural scoliosis from postural curvatures that correct with positioning."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
