          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 2-month-old infant fails the Barlow test (the hip is dislocatable). Which imag</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 2-month-old infant fails the Barlow test (the hip is dislocatable). Which imaging modality should be ordered FIRST to evaluate the hip?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Standing anteroposterior pelvic radiograph</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Plain radiographs are unreliable before 4–6 months because the femoral head is entirely cartilaginous and invisible on X-ray. Radiographs become the primary modality only after the ossific nucleus appears.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>MRI of both hips under sedation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>MRI provides excellent soft-tissue detail but requires sedation in infants and is reserved for pre-operative planning in complex cases, not for initial evaluation of DDH.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>CT scan with three-dimensional reconstruction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CT involves significant ionising radiation and is not indicated as the initial investigation for DDH. It may be used for pre-operative planning in older children with complex hip pathology.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Ultrasound of both hips</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Before 4–6 months the femoral head is cartilaginous and invisible on radiographs. Ultrasound visualises both bony and cartilaginous structures, enabling Graf classification (alpha angle ≥60° is normal).</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In infants younger than 4–6 months the femoral head is entirely cartilaginous and invisible on radiographs. Ultrasound is the first-line investigation, allowing acetabular morphology assessment via the Graf classification (alpha angle ≥60° = normal)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The age of the child and the state of ossification determine imaging choice — cartilaginous structures are invisible on X-ray, making ultrasound essential in the early months of life."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
